### 1. Components Organization & Structure

#### Directory Structure & Hierarchy
Components are stored on disk under the vault path:
`D:\My Drive\My Obsidian Vault\Main_03\Hazard_Trigger_Data\Components\`
(Additionally, hierarchical Matrix components reside under `D:\My Drive\My Obsidian Vault\Main_03\Hazard_Trigger_Data\Matrices\`).

The directory hierarchy follows a 2-tier folder-and-file convention:
1. **Folder Level (Domain / Section):** Subdirectories prefixed with numbered sort keys (e.g., `_01_Subject`, `_02_Appearance`, `_03_Apparel`, `_04_Pose_Action`, `_05_Expression`, `_06_Camera_Framing`, `_07_Background`, `_08_Lighting_Atmosphere`, `_09_Art_Style`, `_10_Engine_Exclusions`).
2. **File Level (Component Target):** JSON data files within folders (e.g., `_01_Subject/1_subjects.json`, `_01_Subject/3_focus.json`, `_07_Background/background.json`, `_07_Background/setting.json`). Files starting with an underscore (such as `_components_meta.json` or `_schema.json`) are treated as system/metadata files and ignored by option scanners.

#### Metadata Registry
Folder and file visual presentation, order, icons, and purpose descriptions are enriched via `D:\My Drive\My Obsidian Vault\Main_03\Hazard_Trigger_Data\Components\_components_meta.json` (fallback: `_meta.json`).

#### Core Data Structures
From `D:\My Drive\My Obsidian Vault\Main_03\.obsidian\plugins\obsidian-hazard-trigger\src\utils\componentScanner.ts`:

```typescript
export interface ComponentEntry {
  display_name: string;
  prompt: string;
  keywords?: string[];
  require?: string[];
  negate?: string[];
  exclude?: string[];
  inherit?: string[];
  subjects?: [number, number]; // [females, males]
  emotions?: string[];
  postures?: string[];
  locked?: boolean;            // Excluded from standard random/gacha unless unlocked
  lockKey?: string;
  completed?: boolean;         // Verified completion flag
  tag_team?: string[];         // Inherited component Hex IDs
  'anti-prompt'?: string;      // Negative prompt subtractions
  requirement?: string[];      // Precondition condition Hex IDs
  impact?: string[];           // State assertion condition Hex IDs
  [key: string]: any;
}

export interface ComponentFileNode {
  fileName: string;
  displayName: string;
  relPath: string; // e.g. "_01_Subject/1_subjects.json"
  meta?: ComponentFileMetadata;
  entries: Record<string, ComponentEntry>;
}

export interface ComponentFolderNode {
  folderName: string;
  displayName: string;
  relPath: string; // e.g. "_01_Subject"
  meta?: ComponentFolderMetadata;
  files: ComponentFileNode[];
}
```

#### Scanning and Loading Pattern
`ComponentScanner.scanAll(app: App)` in `D:\My Drive\My Obsidian Vault\Main_03\.obsidian\plugins\obsidian-hazard-trigger\src\utils\componentScanner.ts`:
1. Reads `_components_meta.json` into a lookup registry.
2. Iterates all directories under `Hazard_Trigger_Data/Components/`.
3. Reads all `.json` files inside each directory (skipping `_*` files).
4. Parses each JSON file into `Record<string, ComponentEntry>`.
5. Maps display names using metadata registry with resilient key lookups; falls back to cleaning file/folder names (stripping prefixes and `.json`, title-casing).
6. Sorts folders and files first by `meta.order` ascending, then naturally by name.

---

### 2. Conditions Storage & Architecture

#### Storage Location
- Root Directory: `D:\My Drive\My Obsidian Vault\Main_03\Hazard_Trigger_Data\Conditions\`
- Primary File: `D:\My Drive\My Obsidian Vault\Main_03\Hazard_Trigger_Data\Conditions\conditions.json`

#### Data Structure
From `D:\My Drive\My Obsidian Vault\Main_03\.obsidian\plugins\obsidian-hazard-trigger\src\types\schema.ts`:

```typescript
export interface ConditionEntry {
  display_name: string;
  category: string;           // E.g. "Pose", "Subject", "Environment", "Camera"
  description: string;
  completed?: boolean;
  tag_team?: string[];        // Array of condition Hex IDs bundled/implied
  conflicts_with?: string[];  // Array of condition Hex IDs that collide with this condition
}
```

Entries are stored as a key-value dictionary keyed by Hex IDs (generated via `generateHexId()`):
```json
{
  "1995b021a4f": {
    "display_name": "Pose Standing",
    "category": "Pose",
    "description": "Character standing upright on two feet with body elevated.",
    "completed": true,
    "conflicts_with": [
      "1995b022b10",
      "1995b023c21",
      "1995b024d32"
    ]
  }
}
```

#### File I/O and Sanitization (`ConditionManager`)
From `D:\My Drive\My Obsidian Vault\Main_03\.obsidian\plugins\obsidian-hazard-trigger\src\utils\conditionManager.ts`:
- **Load / Multi-file Merge:** `loadAllConditions(app: App)` scans all `.json` files in `Hazard_Trigger_Data/Conditions/` and merges their entries into a single dictionary.
- **Sanitization:** `sanitizeCondition(raw: any)` enforces the V2 schema, stripping legacy keys and ensuring `tag_team` and `conflicts_with` are clean string arrays.
- **Save:** `saveAllConditions(app, conditions, targetFileName = 'conditions.json')` writes sanitized JSON back to `Hazard_Trigger_Data/Conditions/conditions.json` with 2-space indentation.
- **Single Save / Delete:** `saveCondition(app, id, entry)` and `deleteCondition(app, id)` follow a load-modify-save pattern.

#### Collision & Physics Engine Integration
In `useTriggerStore.ts` (`calculateState`):
- Active components assert conditions through `impact` (and legacy `keywords`).
- Conditions expand recursively via `tag_team`.
- The engine cross-references active conditions against `conflicts_with` arrays and reports `ConditionConflict` pairs to the UI banner if mutually conflicting conditions are active.

---

### 3. The "CATEGORIES" Concept

The codebase uses "Categories" across several distinct tiers:

#### A. Condition Categories
- `ConditionEntry.category` groups conditions into taxonomy domains (e.g., `Subject`, `Pose`, `Apparel`, `Environment`, `Camera`, `Expression`, `Lighting`, `Art Style`, `General`).
- `ConditionsManagerView.tsx` (`PRESET_CATEGORIES`) provides tab filters with dynamic counts.
- `NotionConditionMultiSelect.tsx` groups multi-select entries by category headers and supports category filtering (`cat:Pose`, `cat:Subject`).

#### B. Notion Category Selector & Dynamic Styling
In `D:\My Drive\My Obsidian Vault\Main_03\.obsidian\plugins\obsidian-hazard-trigger\src\app\components\NotionCategorySelect.tsx`:
- Provides an inline Notion-style searchable dropdown for assigning or creating categories on the fly (`handleCreateNew`).
- `getCategoryBadgeStyle(category)` maps category names to color-coded badge CSS classes:
  - `subject` -> Amber
  - `pose` / `action` -> Cyan
  - `apparel` / `clothing` / `outfit` -> Purple
  - `environment` / `background` / `setting` -> Emerald
  - `camera` / `framing` / `angle` -> Sky
  - `expression` / `mood` / `emotion` -> Pink
  - `lighting` / `atmosphere` -> Yellow
  - `style` / `art` / `quality` -> Fuchsia
  - Default -> Indigo

#### C. Component File Categories in Metadata
In `_components_meta.json`, `files[<relPath>].category` organizes component files by logical domain (e.g., `"Subject Core"`, `"Attention & Composition"`, `"Hair & Head"`, `"Eyes & Vision"`, `"Anatomy"`, `"Biology"`, `"Apparel"`, `"Pose"`, `"Expression"`, `"Camera"`, `"Environment"`, `"Atmosphere"`, `"Style"`, `"Engine"`).

#### D. Template Categories
In `schema.ts` (`HazardTemplate.category`), templates are classified into folders/categories (e.g., `General`, `Eva`, `Outfit Sets`, `Legacy`).

#### E. Studio / Legacy Category Interface
In `schema.ts` / `vaultIo.ts` / `defaultData.ts`, `Category` represents a top-level prompt node group:
```typescript
export interface Category {
  id: string;
  name: string;
  icon: string;
  color: string;
  order: number;
  description?: string;
  selectionType: SelectionType;
  maxSelections?: number;
}
```

